# ── Invite gate (estimate 1.1) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# One shared code per market: "<code>:<market_id>", comma separated.
# Omit to fall back to the built-in defaults (sgv-founding, pasadena).
SEED_INVITE_CODES=sgv-founding:pasadena,pasadena:pasadena

# ── Database (Supabase Postgres) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Use the **pooler** host, not the direct one: Supabase's direct host is
# IPv6-only without the paid add-on, and an IPv4 VPS cannot reach it. The pooler
# is IPv4 and looks like:
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
#
# Port 6543 is transaction mode; lib/server/db.ts disables prepared statements
# for it. Port 5432 on the same host is session mode and also works.
#
# Leave this empty and the app still runs end to end: every write route answers
# `persisted: false` and no screen claims a contribution was stored.
DATABASE_URL=

# ── Extraction (estimate 1.8/1.9) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Scores each contribution's free text 0-1 and raises a flag when it looks like
# it names a person. Unset ⇒ no extraction runs, `confidence` stays null, and
# the admin shows "no extraction run yet" — a wrong score is worse than none,
# because it would sort a card out of the very queue meant to catch it.
#
# Never reads a caregiver's restricted notes (invariant 12), and never logs the
# text it scores (invariant 7).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── Admin (estimate 2.1) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials live in the **database** (`admin_users`, migration 0008). Nothing
# below is needed once somebody is in that table:
#
#     npm run admin:user -- add janet      # prints a passphrase to hand over once
#     npm run admin:user -- list
#     npm run admin:user -- password janet # rotate; ends their sessions
#     npm run admin:user -- disable janet  # revoke, without a deploy
#
# Run from a checkout, like migrations — against this machine's DATABASE_URL, or
# another deployment's via MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL.
#
# A populated table is authoritative: the variables below are **not** consulted,
# so someone revoked there cannot be let back in by an entry nobody remembered to
# remove. If the table cannot be read, sign-in is refused rather than falling
# back — the admin has nothing to show without that database anyway.
#
# The admin does not exist until the table or one of the two modes below has
# somebody in it — an unprotected admin is a worse failure than an unavailable one.
#
# BOOTSTRAP — one scrypt record per person, same hash, in an env var. This is what
# admits the first person on a deployment whose table is still empty. Generate:
#
#     npm run admin:credential -- janet
#
# It prints a passphrase to hand over once, and the record to paste here. Repeat
# per person and join the records with commas.
ADMIN_CREDENTIALS=
#
# Set this in production. With it, rotating one person's password ends only that
# person's sessions; without it the key is derived from the credentials in force
# (the table, or the records above), so rotating anyone's password signs everyone
# out. Any long random string.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=
#
# DEPRECATED — the original shared password, read only when ADMIN_CREDENTIALS is
# empty. In this mode the name on an audit row is *picked from a list*, not proved:
# anyone holding the password can sign in as anyone, which defeats the point of
# attributing a caregiver decision to a person. Kept so an existing deployment does
# not go dark on the deploy that adds ADMIN_CREDENTIALS. Delete both once you have
# moved; the sign-in screen warns while they are in use.


# ── Analytics (estimate 3.1 / M3) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset ⇒ `lib/posthog-provider.tsx` initialises nothing and every `track()` call
# is a no-op. Autocapture and session recording are off in the provider and must
# stay off (invariant 7 — see CLAUDE.md Decisions).
#
# ⚠️ These two are read at BUILD time, not request time: Next inlines any
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` into the client bundle during `next build`. Locally that means
# restarting `npm run dev` after changing them. In production it means they are
# Docker build args passed by CI from repo secrets — putting them in
# /docker/pando/.env does nothing. See DEPLOY.md §3.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
#
# Screen recording (session replay). Set to 1 to switch it on; anything else,
# including unset, leaves it off — and off is the product's position, not a
# placeholder. Replay sends the *contents of the screen*, and this flow renders a
# child's birth year, their school, the neighborhood and free text about a named
# caregiver — with the profile review, the chat transcript and /done each showing
# every earlier answer at once, so there is no later screen that is safe to start
# from. It exists so the client can be watched walking the flow during QA.
# Unset it before the first real founding contributor, same deadline as
# SEED_VERIFY_DEV_CODES and the `pando` starter password.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_SESSION_RECORDING=

# Server-side, request-time: the dashboard the admin overview links to.
# Optional — the link is hidden when unset.
POSTHOG_DASHBOARD_URL=

# ── Phone verification + SMS (client's v3.2 round) ───────────────────────────
# The founding path holds the profile and every card on the parent's phone until a
# six-digit code is confirmed; only then is anything sent. Set to 0 to switch the
# gate off for a local walkthrough or a pre-A2P demo (writes then behave as before).
SEED_REQUIRE_VERIFICATION=1
# Signs the pending-verification cookie. Falls back to ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET.
SEED_VERIFY_SECRET=
# Testing only: returns the code in the API response and prints it on screen, so
# the whole flow can be walked while the A2P campaign is still with the carriers.
# Set to 1. Works on the server too (14 Aug) — pilot-scale testing runs there, and
# without it the deployed app can store nothing until Twilio is provisioned.
#
# The OTP itself is unchanged: 6 digits, 5 minutes, 3 sends, 3 guesses, 15-minute
# lock. What it removes is proof that the parent holds the number — so a `consents`
# row written while this is on is not evidence, and /admin/consents is the A2P §3.3
# defence file. Unset it before the first real founding contributor.
SEED_VERIFY_DEV_CODES=
# Twilio. All three are required before a single text can go out, and sends always
# use the Messaging Service — never a bare number (client's instruction).
# Blocked on: A2P 10DLC campaign approval + the Messaging Service SID.
#
# The app accepts US (+1) and Ukrainian (+380) numbers (20 Aug). Twilio does not
# treat them alike: A2P 10DLC is a US registration, so a +380 recipient needs Geo
# Permissions enabled for Ukraine on the account *and* a sender the Messaging
# Service can use outside the US. Nothing here changes for that — it is console
# configuration, and until it is done a +380 number gets `provider_error` while a
# +1 one sends. Dev codes are how a Ukrainian tester walks the flow meanwhile.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=

# The public URL Twilio calls, used to verify the inbound webhook's signature
# (13.2). Configuration and never a host header: behind Traefik that header is
# whatever reached the container, so whoever can set it could choose the URL the
# signature is checked against. No trailing slash. Unset means every inbound
# request is refused — the endpoint fails closed rather than running unverified.
TWILIO_WEBHOOK_BASE_URL=https://pando.is

# Two webhooks to point at that base, both signed and both refusing an
# unverified request:
#   POST /api/sms/inbound   every inbound text (13.2) — STOP/START/HELP are
#                           handled here, before any classifier sees them
#   POST /api/sms/status    delivery status (13.4) — set as the Messaging
#                           Service's status callback, or message_log.status
#                           stays null and the delivery rate cannot be computed

# M9.5 — the bearer token the host cron presents to /api/jobs/run.
#
# The jobs send texts and grant credits, so unset means every run is refused —
# the same fail-closed rule as the Twilio signature. Calling too often is safe:
# whether a job is due is answered from job_runs, and a second attempt while one
# is in flight is refused by a unique index rather than racing it, so a retry, a
# restart mid-run and two overlapping hosts are all the same event.
#
#   0  *  *  *  *  curl -fsS -X POST -H "authorization: Bearer $JOBS_SECRET" \
#                    "https://pando.is/api/jobs/run?job=expire_blasts"
#   15 9  *  *  *  ... "?job=delivery_check"
#   30 10 *  *  *  ... "?job=freshness_ping"
#
# GET the same URL to list the jobs and their intervals without reading code.
# freshness_ping is the only one that texts anybody; the other two are reads.
JOBS_SECRET=

# ── The Slack relay — a temporary test transport, not a product feature ───────
#
# Swaps Slack in for Twilio for **everything except verification**, so answers,
# Network Asks, freshness pings, captures and the thanks loop can all be
# exercised in one channel before a real parent is texted (2 Sep). M5, M7, M9 and
# M10 are built and none of them has run end to end, because the only way to see
# what Pando says was to have it text somebody.
#
# It replaces exactly one step. `sendSms` keeps its whole order — opt-out, quiet
# hours, contributor protection, *then* the provider (invariant 6) — and only the
# provider changes, which is what makes testing through it worth anything.
#
# `MESSAGING_RELAY=slack` is the switch; unset means off. A half-configured relay
# (switch on, no token) falls back to the real provider rather than silently
# swallowing every message.
MESSAGING_RELAY=

# A bot token (xoxb-…) with `chat:write`, and the channel id (not its name) it
# posts to. Every post names its recipient with a **masked** number — one channel
# holding every message needs to say who each one was for, and a full number in a
# channel is the one thing invariant 7 still refuses here.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
SLACK_CHANNEL_ID=

# Signs the Events API requests to POST /api/slack/events. Unset means every
# inbound event is refused — the same fail-closed rule as the Twilio webhook,
# and it matters more here because Slack's scheme also carries a replay window.
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# The bot's own user id, so the events route ignores what Pando itself posted.
# Without it Pando answers its own messages, which is an infinite loop that would
# spend every contributor's monthly allowance in seconds. `event.bot_id` catches
# it too; this is belt and braces for a workspace where the bot posts as a user.
SLACK_BOT_USER_ID=
#
# Two ways a channel message becomes a message from a parent:
#   in a thread          — a reply under one of Pando's posts is from whoever
#                          that post was addressed to (the thread's `ts` is the
#                          message's `provider_message_id`)
#   +16265550143: hello  — a top-level message naming a number, which is the only
#                          way to test 5.9's cold inbound, since a stranger has
#                          no thread yet by definition
# Anything else in the channel is ignored: a human talking is not an inbound text.
#
# ⚠️ Never enable this against real contributors. One channel holding every
# message Pando sends is a transcript of the network. It comes off before the
# pilot opens — the same deadline as SEED_VERIFY_DEV_CODES and the `pando`
# starter password.

# Points the relay's posts at a local stub instead of Slack, so the sending path
# can be exercised (`npm run test:relay-live`). Same purpose and same warning as
# TWILIO_API_BASE: never set in production.
SLACK_API_BASE=

# ── Stripe (M13.5–13.7) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unset ⇒ no payment link can be created and no refund can be made, and
# `/admin/payments` says so at the top of the page. The same honesty rule as
# `persisted: false` and the SMS layer's `not_provisioned`: never pretend.
#
# The prices are NOT here. They live in `lib/blast-tiers.ts` — Board Ask $5,
# Targeted Ask $15, from the 8.18 strategy — and a checkout sends the amount
# inline rather than referencing a Stripe Price object. That is deliberate: a
# Price in the dashboard would be a second copy of a number this codebase owns,
# editable by somebody who cannot see the code, and it would silently disagree
# with the amount a refund reads back.
#
# Note the estimate (13.5) asks for "$5 / $12 / $20 / $35". Those are its own
# older five-tier numbers, unchanged between the 26 Aug and 3 Sep workbooks, and
# CLAUDE.md's rule is that the newer client document wins. Changing them is one
# edit in `lib/blast-tiers.ts`, never a migration.
#
# `sk_test_…` puts the whole page in test mode and it says so loudly — a screen
# of plausible payments that all happened in a sandbox is the most misleading
# state this surface can be in.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The webhook signing secret, from the Stripe dashboard's endpoint page. It is a
# *different* secret from the API key and has its own variable for that reason.
#
# ⚠️ **Set this in the same deploy as the key, never later.** Unset means the
# webhook refuses every delivery — fail-closed, like the Twilio signature and
# `JOBS_SECRET` — so a parent could pay and their Ask would sit unpaid forever
# with nothing in the logs but a 400. `/admin/payments` paints that state red.
#
# The endpoint to register is `POST /api/stripe/webhook`, subscribed to
# `checkout.session.completed`, `checkout.session.expired`,
# `checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded` and
# `checkout.session.async_payment_failed`. Anything else it receives is answered
# 200 and ignored, so extra subscriptions are harmless rather than noisy.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Where a parent lands after paying, and where the checkout's cancel link goes.
# Read from TWILIO_WEBHOOK_BASE_URL (or NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL) rather than from a
# host header — for the same reason the Twilio verifier does: a return URL built
# from a header is a header somebody else can set, and here it would send a
# paying parent to another site.

# ── Rate limiting (M15.4) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# How many reverse proxies sit between the internet and this container.
# **Default 1**, which is correct for the VPS as it stands: Traefik owns 80/443
# and forwards to the app (CLAUDE.md, 5 Aug).
#
# ## Why this is a variable and not a constant
#
# A proxy **appends** the address it saw to any `X-Forwarded-For` the request
# arrived with. So the header this app receives looks like
#
#     <whatever the caller made up>, <the address Traefik actually saw>
#
# and the caller's real address is the **last** entry — not the first, which is
# the one every tutorial reaches for and which is entirely under the caller's
# control. `lib/rate-limits.ts` counts from the right, one hop per trusted
# proxy, and `npm run test:security` pins that a forged leading entry cannot
# bypass a limit.
#
# Get this number wrong in the *high* direction and the limiter reads a
# caller-supplied value again — the same bypass. So:
#
#   1  Traefik only (today)
#   2  Cloudflare (or any CDN) in front of Traefik
#   0  the app exposed directly, with no proxy at all — the socket address is
#      then all there is, and `x-forwarded-for` should not be trusted at all
#
# The counters are in-process, which is adequate because there is one container.
# The day this runs as two, each enforces its own copy and the effective ceiling
# doubles — that is the moment to move the buckets into Postgres, and `consume`
# is pure precisely so the store is the only thing that changes.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=1
